Camera: do not hold the device lock while closing the backend.

Closing a camera deadlocks the calling thread on any backend that drives SDL
from the platform's own frame callback -- Android and Apple, both of which set
ProvidesOwnCallbackThread. The application stays alive with every thread
asleep, and never draws again, because the thread parked is the one that
draws.

Caught with a debugger attached to a frozen application on a Pixel 5a. Two
threads, each waiting for the other:

    thread 'SDLThread'                     -- the closer
      schultz_camera_close
      ClosePhysicalCamera       SDL_camera.c:245
      ANDROIDCAMERA_CloseDevice SDL_camera_android.c:490
      ... AImageReader_delete, waiting for the callback to stop

    thread 'ImageReader-326'               -- the callback
      SDL_CameraThreadIterate   SDL_camera.c:914
      ... blocked on SDL_LockMutex(device->lock)

SDL_CameraThreadIterate takes device->lock twice: once around acquiring the
frame, and again at the end to put the finished surface on the filled list.
Between the two it converts the picture, which for a large frame is most of
its running time. ClosePhysicalCamera held that same lock across CloseDevice,
and CloseDevice must get the platform to stop calling back -- on Android by
deleting the image reader, which waits for the callback to return. A callback
converting when the close began reaches its second lock, blocks on the lock
the closer holds, and the closer waits for that callback for ever.

The window is the conversion time, which is linear in pixels: at 2448x3264 it
is nearly every close, at 1280x960 it is occasional, and at 1024x768 it
effectively never happens. That size dependence is what makes it read as a
memory fault rather than as a lock.

The fix releases the lock before calling the backend's CloseDevice and takes
it again afterwards. That is safe because the shutdown flag is already set by
then: a callback arriving from that point takes the lock, sees the flag and
returns without touching anything, and one already past its first lock is past
AcquireFrame -- which runs under the lock -- and from there touches only
surfaces and its own pixels, never the backend's private data being freed.
Ref counting is unchanged; ReleaseCamera and ObtainPhysicalCameraObj pair.

Note that an earlier attempt at this -- checking the shutdown flag before
taking the lock at the top of SDL_CameraThreadIterate -- does not fix it. The
blocked callback is at the *second* lock, long past that check.

This is what the FIXME already sitting on ClosePhysicalCamera has in mind:

    // !!! FIXME: the close_cond stuff from audio might help the race
    // condition here.

Applies to SDL3-3.4.16. Drop this file when the fix is upstream.

--- a/src/camera/SDL_camera.c
+++ b/src/camera/SDL_camera.c
@@ -261,7 +261,36 @@
         }
     }
 
+    // Without the lock, and this is the whole of the bug it fixes.
+    //
+    // A backend that provides its own callback thread -- Android and Apple
+    // both do -- runs SDL_CameraThreadIterate from the platform's frame
+    // callback, and that function takes this lock twice: once around
+    // acquiring the frame, and again at the end to put the finished surface
+    // on the filled list. Between the two it converts the picture, which for
+    // a large frame is most of its running time.
+    //
+    // Closing held this lock across CloseDevice, and CloseDevice has to get
+    // the platform to stop calling back -- on Android by deleting the image
+    // reader, which waits for the callback to return. So a callback that was
+    // converting when the close began would reach its second lock, block on
+    // the lock the closer holds, and the closer would wait for that callback
+    // forever. Both threads asleep, the application alive and never drawing
+    // again. Observed on a Pixel 5a: the closer in AImageReader_delete, the
+    // callback at the second SDL_LockMutex in SDL_CameraThreadIterate.
+    //
+    // Releasing it first is safe because the shutdown flag is already set
+    // above. A callback arriving from here on takes the lock, sees the flag
+    // and returns without touching anything. One already past its first lock
+    // is past AcquireFrame -- which runs under the lock -- and from there
+    // touches only surfaces and its own pixels, never the backend's private
+    // data that CloseDevice is freeing. The surfaces themselves are freed
+    // below, after the lock is taken again, so that callback's last act and
+    // this teardown stay in order.
+    ReleaseCamera(device);
     camera_driver.impl.CloseDevice(device);
+    ObtainPhysicalCameraObj(device);
+
     device->hidden = NULL;  // just in case backend didn't reset this.
 
     SDL_DestroyProperties(device->props);
